<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanis was an ancient city in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> it became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smendes">Smendes</a>, who controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> while the High Priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom cite this division as evidence that the New Kingdom ended through administrative dissolution rather than conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanis.jpg" alt="Tanis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Markh at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_Tanis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tanis, the Greek name of the ancient Egyptian ḏꜥn.t, is also known as San al-Hagar. It is an important archaeological site in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, on the Tanitic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, which has long since silted up. The city was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the 21st and 22nd Dynasties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tanis is unattested before the 19th Dynasty, when it was the capital of the 14th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>. It is securely attested in two documents of the 20th Dynasty, the Onomasticon of Amenope and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a>, as the home of the future pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smendes">Smendes</a>. The earliest known buildings there date to the 21st Dynasty. Monuments of earlier date were mostly brought from nearby cities, above all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, the royal residence that was abandoned at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> when the Pelusiac branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> silted up and its harbour became unusable.</p><p>The kings of the 21st Dynasty and later the 22nd, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>, ruled from Tanis. They supported their legitimacy with traditional titles and building, though the works are modest beside those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. They built and enlarged the Great Temple of Amun-Ra, with minor temples to Mut and Khonsu, and scholars call Tanis the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The royal necropolis at Tanis replaced the one in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>.</p><p>After the 22nd Dynasty Tanis lost its royal status and became capital of the 19th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>. Building revived from the 30th Dynasty through the Ptolemaic period. The city was populated until it was abandoned in Roman times. In Late Antiquity it was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavation began in 1825 with Jean-Jacques Rifaud, who found two pink granite sphinxes now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Auguste Mariette dug there from 1860 to 1864 and Flinders Petrie from 1883 to 1886. In 1866 Karl Richard Lepsius found a copy of the Canopus Decree, whose full hieroglyphic text allowed a direct comparison with the Greek and confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Champollion</a>'s approach to deciphering hieroglyphs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> worked at the site from 1929 to 1956. In 1939 and 1940 he found the intact burials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a>, Amenemope and Shoshenq II, with gold jewellery, precious stones and funerary masks. They are the only intact royal pharaonic burials known.</p><p>Because so many monuments bore the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, archaeologists long believed Tanis was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, and the Year 400 Stela led some to identify it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>. The later discovery of Qantir and Tell el-Dab'a showed that these were separate sites. More than twenty fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> mark the Great Temple of Amun-Ra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By approximately 1080 BCE, the High Priest of Amun Herihor governed Upper Egypt as a de facto independent ruler, while Smendes controlled Lower Egypt from <b>Tanis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The country was divided: the Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt ruled the north from <b>Tanis</b> while the high priests held the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/theocracy-of-amun-at-thebes">Theocracy of Amun at Thebes</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital moved to <b>Tanis</b> in the north-eastern Delta, built largely from monuments carried from the abandoned Ramesside capital Pi-Ramesses.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Manetho linked the dynasty with <b>Tanis</b>, while earlier scholars placed it at Leontopolis; many Egyptologists now see it as a mainly Theban and Upper Egyptian line, but its extent is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-third-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-third Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
